{{short description|None}}

{{For|a full account of the club's history|History of Celtic F.C. (disambiguation){{!}}History of Celtic F.C.}}
{{Use dmy dates|date=February 2021}}
[[File:Celtic FC trophy case.JPG|thumb|right|Some of the trophies won by Celtic FC]]
[[Celtic F.C.|Celtic Football Club]] is a Scottish professional [[association football]] club based in [[Parkhead]], [[Glasgow]]. The club was founded in 1887 and played their first match in May 1888, a [[Exhibition game|friendly match]] against [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]].<ref name="brief">{{cite web |url=http://www.celticfc.net/pages/history |title=Brief history |website=Celtic FC |access-date=11 May 2016 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160510143304/http://www.celticfc.net/pages/history |archive-date=10 May 2016 }}</ref> The club played their first competitive match in September 1888, when they entered the first round of the [[1888–89 Scottish Cup]], and were founding members of the [[Scottish Football League]] in 1890 from which they have remained in the [[List of top-division football clubs in UEFA countries|top tier]] of ever since.<ref>{{cite web |last1=Stokkermans |first1=Karel |title=Coventric! |url=https://www.rsssf.org/miscellaneous/coventric.html |website=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |access-date=25 July 2018}}</ref> Celtic have won the [[Scottish football league system|Scottish league title]] on a record 56 occasions, the [[Scottish Cup]] 43 times and the [[Scottish League Cup]] 22 times.<ref name="about">{{cite web|title=About Celtic|url=http://www.celticfc.net/pages/about|website=Celtic FC|access-date=11 May 2016}}</ref> The club enjoyed their greatest successes during the 1960s and 1970s under [[Jock Stein]] when they won [[Nine in a row|nine consecutive league titles]] and the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Jock Stein - the man who made the modern-day Celtic |url=http://www.celticfc.net/news/9047 |website=Celtic FC |access-date=25 July 2018 |date=10 September 2017 |first=Joe |last=Sullivan}}</ref>

As of the end of the [[2024–25 Celtic F.C. season|2024–25 season]], the club have played more than 130 seasons in [[Football in Scotland|Scottish football]]. The table details their achievements and the top goalscorer in senior major first-team competitions to the end of the most recently completed season. Details of the partially completed league campaign in the [[1939–40 in Scottish football|1939–40 season]], abandoned due to the [[World War II|Second World War]], are also included.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/index.php |title=Welcome to Celtic@FitbaStats |work=FitbaStats - Celtic |access-date=25 June 2013}}</ref>

==History==
Celtic played their first ever competitive match on 1 September 1888, a first round [[Scottish Cup]] tie against [[Shettleston F.C. (1879)|Shettleston]], winning 5–1,<ref>{{cite book|last1=Cuddihy|first1=Paul|last2=Friel|first2=David|title=The Century Bhoys: The Official History of Celtic's Greatest Goalscorers|year=2010|publisher=Black & White Publishing|isbn=9781845026103|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=2hsjAwAAQBAJ&dq=celtic%27s+first+ever+competitive+game&pg=PT5}}</ref> and eventually reaching the final where they lost to [[Third Lanark A.C.|Third Lanark]] after a replay.<ref name="s8889">{{cite web|title=Results For Season 1888/1889|url=https://www.fitbastats.com/celtic/team_results_season.php?from=105&competition=0&update=Update|website=FitbaStats - Celtic|access-date=19 December 2017}}</ref> The team also made their debuts in the [[Glasgow Cup]] and the [[Glasgow North Eastern Cup|North-Eastern Cup]] that same season, reaching the semi-final of the Glasgow Cup where they lost to [[Queen's Park F.C.|Queens Park]],<ref name="s8889"/> and defeating [[Cowlairs F.C.|Cowlairs]] 6–1 in the final of the North-Eastern Cup to win their first ever trophy.<ref name="brief" /><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.thecelticwiki.com/m/page/1889-05-11%3A+Celtic+6-1+Cowlairs%2C+Glasgow+North-Eastern+Football+Association+Cup |title=Glasgow North-eastern Football Association Final Cup Tie |work=The Scotsman |page=5 |date=13 May 1889 }}{{Dead link|date=March 2026 |bot=InternetArchiveBot }}</ref> The [[Scottish Football League]] was formed in 1890, and Celtic were among the founding members,<ref>{{cite web|title=The Scottish Football League 1890 - 1891|url=https://www.historicalkits.co.uk/Scottish_Football_League/season/1890-91.htm|website=Historical Football Kits|access-date=24 January 2018}}</ref> finishing third behind joint-winner's [[Rangers F.C.|Rangers]] and [[Dumbarton F.C.|Dumbarton]] in the competition's [[1890–91 Scottish Football League|inaugural season]].<ref>{{cite web|title=SFL Archive 1890/1891|url=https://spfl.co.uk/premiership/archive/1890-1891|website=SPFL|access-date=24 January 2018}}</ref>

In [[1890–91 in Scottish football|1891]], Celtic won their second trophy when they beat Third Lanark 4–0 in the final of the Glasgow Cup.<ref>{{cite web |title=Glasgow Cup Finals 1888-2017 |url=http://www.glasgowfa.co.uk/assets/gc_all_finals.pdf |website=Glasgow FA |access-date=21 June 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180621194142/http://www.glasgowfa.co.uk/assets/gc_all_finals.pdf |archive-date=21 June 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref> The following year saw Celtic winning their first major tournament, defeating [[Queen's Park F.C.|Queen's Park]] 5–1 in the [[1892 Scottish Cup Final]].<ref name="about" /> A [[1892–93 Scottish Football League|first league title]] then followed in season [[1892–93 in Scottish football|1892–93]].<ref name="about" /> The Glasgow Cup remained a prestigious tournament for some years, but declined in importance after the [[Second World War]] following the introduction of the [[Scottish League Cup]] and [[UEFA competitions|European club competitions]].<ref>{{cite web |title=History |url=http://www.glasgowfa.co.uk/history.html |website=Glasgow FA |access-date=25 July 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180725153703/http://www.glasgowfa.co.uk/history.html |archive-date=25 July 2018 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Celtic have since gone on to win the Scottish League Championship on a record 56 occasions, including a run of [[Nine in a row|nine consecutive titles]] in the 1960s, 1970s and the 2010s, the Scottish Cup 42 times, and the Scottish League Cup 22 times.<ref name="about" />

Celtic first participated in [[Celtic F.C. in European football|European competition]] during the [[1962–63 Celtic F.C. season|1962–63 season]], their third-place finish in the league the previous season qualifying them for the [[Inter-Cities Fairs Cup]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Celtic in Europe 1961-64 |url=http://www.football50.co.uk/celtic-in-europe-1961-64/ |website=Jim Craig's Football 50 |access-date=20 June 2018}}</ref> They went on to reach two [[UEFA Cup Winner's Cup|European Cup Winner's Cup]] semi-finals in [[1963–64 European Cup Winners' Cup|1964]] and [[1965–66 European Cup Winners' Cup|1966]],<ref name="uefa">{{cite web |title=Celtic FC - History |url=http://www.uefa.com/teamsandplayers/teams/club=50050/profile/history/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150927091924/http://www.uefa.com/teamsandplayers/teams/club=50050/profile/history/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=27 September 2015 |website=[[UEFA]] |access-date=20 June 2018}}</ref> before going on to become the first British club to win the [[UEFA Champions League|European Cup]] in [[1966–67 European Cup|1967]], defeating [[Inter Milan]] 2–1 in [[1967 European Cup Final|the final]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Barham|first=Albert|title=Relentless attack captures European Cup|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/1967/may/25/championsleague.sport|access-date=27 June 2014|newspaper=The Guardian|date=26 May 1967}}</ref> Celtic reached the European Cup Final again in [[1969–70 European Cup|1970]], but lost to [[Feyenoord]].<ref>{{cite news |last1=Green |first1=Geoffrey |title=Dutch humble Celtic to take cup |url=http://image.wikifoundry.com/image/1/NVYfkSGwIg06HZwwZZaaIg233921/GW570H1250 |access-date=20 June 2018 |work=The Times |date=7 May 1970}}</ref> Their last appearance in a European final was in [[2002–03 Celtic F.C. season|2003]] when they lost 3–2 to [[FC Porto]] in the [[2003 UEFA Cup Final|UEFA Cup Final]].<ref name="fin1">{{cite web|title=Derlei decides dramatic final|url=https://www.uefa.com/uefaeuropaleague/match/71041--celtic-vs-porto/|website=[[UEFA]]|access-date=3 January 2016|date=22 May 2003}}</ref>
<ref>{{cite news |last=Forsyth |first=Roddy |title=Celtic's form has James Forrest dreaming of a European run to rival 2003 |url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/football/2017/12/02/celtics-form-has-james-forrest-dreaming-european-run-rival-2003/ |access-date=20 June 2018 |work=The Telegraph |date=2 December 2007}}</ref>

==Key==
;Key to league record:
{{colbegin|colwidth=22em}}
* Pld – Matches played
* W – Matches won
* D – Matches drawn
* L – Matches lost
* GF – Goals for
* GA – Goals against
* Pts – Points
* Pos – Final position
{{colend}}
;Key to divisions:
{{colbegin|colwidth=25em}}
* SFL – [[Scottish Football League]]
* SPD – [[Scottish Football League Premier Division]]
* SPL – [[Scottish Premier League]]
* SP – [[Scottish Premiership]]
*{{Color box|Moccasin|border=darkgray}} – Top scorer in division<ref>{{cite web |last1=Brown |first1=Alan |last2=Preston |first2=Simon |last3=Di Maggio |first3=Roberto |title=Scotland - List of Topscorers |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tabless/scottops.html |website=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |access-date=25 July 2018}}</ref>
{{colend}}
;Key to rounds
{{colbegin|colwidth=22em}}
*1R – first round, etc.
*QF – quarter-finals
*SF – semi-finals
*{{Color box|#C0C0C0|RU|border=darkgray}} – Runners-up
*{{Color box|gold|'''W'''{{0|ii}}|border=darkgray}} – Winners
{{colend}}

* Goals noted for '''Top Goalscorer''' comprises goals scored in [[Scottish football league system|League]], [[Scottish Cup]], [[Scottish League Cup]], [[Glasgow Cup]] and [[Celtic F.C. in European football|European Club]] competitions.

